\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The integrated model with two memories, at one illustrative setting: the binary-coded encoding, both memories having studied $100+100$ and then $100+200$, the same associations, criterion $1/2$, search length 1, and the same solver, whose single-digit facts come from DOA's separate association table. ACT-R's parameters are a mismatch penalty $\mu = 1/2$ over 24 features and a failure weight $\theta = 1/10$. All values are exact and proved in Lean. ``Correct overall'' is the joint probability that the solver is invoked and is correct: the product of the two rows above it (only the solver ever gives 250). Rows about whole-problem retrieval do not depend on the backup (\texttt  {whole\_independent\_of\_solver}), so they are the same in \texttt  {ExampleACTR.lean}, which uses an exact backup. Rows about answers overall use the integrated model (\texttt  {setting\_routes}, \texttt  {setting\_solver\_x}, \texttt  {setting\_actr\_x}, \texttt  {setting\_practiced\_errors}). EPAM's overall errors on $100+100$ are the solver's own; after studying $100+300$ they are unchanged, by \texttt  {MD.learning\_preserves\_trial}, which holds for every backup that learning leaves fixed, as this solver's table is. The last row states what holds at every setting for this configuration.}}{27}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:memories}{{3}{27}{The integrated model with two memories, at one illustrative setting: the binary-coded encoding, both memories having studied $100+100$ and then $100+200$, the same associations, criterion $1/2$, search length 1, and the same solver, whose single-digit facts come from DOA's separate association table. ACT-R's parameters are a mismatch penalty $\mu = 1/2$ over 24 features and a failure weight $\theta = 1/10$. All values are exact and proved in Lean. ``Correct overall'' is the joint probability that the solver is invoked and is correct: the product of the two rows above it (only the solver ever gives 250). Rows about whole-problem retrieval do not depend on the backup (\lean {whole\_independent\_of\_solver}), so they are the same in \code {ExampleACTR.lean}, which uses an exact backup. Rows about answers overall use the integrated model (\lean {setting\_routes}, \lean {setting\_solver\_x}, \lean {setting\_actr\_x}, \lean {setting\_practiced\_errors}). EPAM's overall errors on $100+100$ are the solver's own; after studying $100+300$ they are unchanged, by \lean {MD.learning\_preserves\_trial}, which holds for every backup that learning leaves fixed, as this solver's table is. The last row states what holds at every setting for this configuration}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The model with one memory for whole problems and column facts (\S  \ref  {sec:shared}). The memory first studies the three digit pairs that $150+100$ and $100+100$ need, then the two problems. The middle column is a control: ACT-R's memory holds the same five items, but the solver uses DOA's separate table, so the memory's contents are as in the last column and only the solver's use of the memory differs. Rows as in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:memories}; ``correct overall'' is again the product of the two rows above it. EPAM's values are those of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:memories}, by the reduction theorem (\texttt  {setting\_epam\_reduces}, \texttt  {setting\_epam\_values}, \texttt  {setting\_epam\_solver}, \texttt  {setting\_00\_not\_5}); ACT-R's are \texttt  {setting\_act\_control}, \texttt  {setting\_act\_pairs} and \texttt  {setting\_act\_values}. ACT-R appears without retrieval as use. The last row is \texttt  {shared\_learning\_preserves\_trial}; without its condition on the digit pairs, noninterference is not proved for the shared model.}}{28}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:shared}{{4}{28}{The model with one memory for whole problems and column facts (\S \ref {sec:shared}). The memory first studies the three digit pairs that $150+100$ and $100+100$ need, then the two problems. The middle column is a control: ACT-R's memory holds the same five items, but the solver uses DOA's separate table, so the memory's contents are as in the last column and only the solver's use of the memory differs. Rows as in Table~\ref {tab:memories}; ``correct overall'' is again the product of the two rows above it. EPAM's values are those of Table~\ref {tab:memories}, by the reduction theorem (\lean {setting\_epam\_reduces}, \lean {setting\_epam\_values}, \lean {setting\_epam\_solver}, \lean {setting\_00\_not\_5}); ACT-R's are \lean {setting\_act\_control}, \lean {setting\_act\_pairs} and \lean {setting\_act\_values}. ACT-R appears without retrieval as use. The last row is \lean {shared\_learning\_preserves\_trial}; without its condition on the digit pairs, noninterference is not proved for the shared model}{table.4}{}}
